| **Your existing silicotuberculosis source** | Ehrlich and colleagues discuss concealment, altered appearances and jointly produced masses, but converting those observations into three numbered regimes remains our interpretation. — [Ehrlich et al., 2024, *European Respiratory Review*, “Imaging” and “Pathology”](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11668511/). |
| **A source distinguishing mechanisms related to the fourth** | The tuberculosis–cancer series separates masking from misinterpretation and lesion subtlety, providing a classification of diagnostic difficulty rather than of all four spatial regimes. — [Kim et al., 2001, *Korean Journal of Radiology*, “Materials and Methods” and Results](https://www.kjronline.org/DOIx.php?id=10.3348/kjr.2001.2.3.138). |

**Our proposed reporting vocabulary**

| Regime | Working name—our proposal, not a published taxonomy | Additional information to record |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Spatial separation with preserved recognition | Location and evidence that both diagnoses are identifiable. |
| 2 | Anatomical overlap with preserved recognition | Evidence for a shared region and independently identifiable components. |
| 3 | Morphological interaction with loss of separability | Evidence of altered morphology and the separate basis for claiming non-decomposability. |
| 4 | Confirmed disease without separate image recognition | Whether the explanation is obscuration, effacement, radiographic occultness or an observer miss. |

