| *The Handbook of Medical Image Perception and Techniques* — Ehsan Samei and Elizabeth A. Krupinski, editors; Kevin Berbaum, Edmund Franken, Robert Caldwell, Kevin Schartz and Mark Madsen, chapter authors | 2nd edition, 2018 | Chapter 10, “Satisfaction of Search in Radiology,” **pp. 121–166** | This dedicated chapter addresses the multiple-abnormality detection problem, with its published bibliography covering chest-radiograph search, chest CT, clinical history and checklists. ([Berbaum et al., 2018, chapter 10, publisher record and bibliography](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/handbook-of-medical-image-perception-and-techniques/satisfaction-of-search-in-radiology/D6DFE5D9B971E52897609D913C0CD960)) | Chapter identity, pages and bibliography verified; full chapter prose was not accessible, so specific instructional recommendations are not attributed to it here. |

The two IDKD entries are separate dated volumes from the same teaching series, rather than independent textbook traditions. ([Springer, *Diseases of the Chest and Heart 2015–2018*, bibliographic information](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-88-470-5752-4))

A particularly direct passage states that coexisting disorders “may obscure or alter the otherwise characteristic radiographic appearance of a pulmonary opacification.” ([Goodman et al., 2015, chapter 8, “Nosocomial Pneumonia—Radiographic Diagnosis”](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7121828/))

The ICU caption is equally explicit about coexistence: “A combination of atelectasis and pneumonia is present in the left lower lobe.” ([Lohan, 2019, chapter 7, Fig. 7.9](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7122429/))

**Our interpretation:** these passages establish that combined appearance and obscuration are already teaching subjects, but they do not establish the proposed four-regime taxonomy.

