| Lloyd, Walsh and Hansell, “High-resolution CT of complications of idiopathic fibrotic lung disease,” *British Journal of Radiology* | Journal review, 2011; **84:581–592** | Sections on infection, malignancy and acute deterioration; Tables 2–4 | The review teaches how to investigate additional disease in fibrotic lungs, including infection, cancer and cardiac oedema, while distinguishing these possibilities from acute exacerbation. ([Lloyd et al., 2011, abstract and sections on acute deterioration](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3473493/)) |
| Cottin et al., “Syndrome of Combined Pulmonary Fibrosis and Emphysema: An Official ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Research Statement,” *American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine* | Research statement, 2022; **206(4):e7–e41** | “Imaging features”; Figs. 1–8 | The statement illustrates separate and admixed emphysema–fibrosis patterns and explains that spatial superimposition can alter their usual CT appearances. ([Cottin et al., 2022, “Imaging features,” Figs. 1–8](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615200/)) |

The CPFE statement provides particularly relevant teaching beyond a simple upper-lung/lower-lung separation, because Figure 7 follows the development of a thick-walled cystic lesion within fibrotic lung over four years. ([Cottin et al., 2022, Fig. 7](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615200/))

